<%# Every number in a calculation, explained, with the values read from the
    released model rather than written out here. A page that restated the
    weights in prose would be a second copy of the config, and the second copy
    is the one that goes stale.

    Anchors are load-bearing: the working on a claim page links each factor to
    its section here, which is how a reader gets from "0.6 × 0.9" to what those
    numbers are (owner, 2026-09-22). %>
<% content_for :title, "How a score is worked out" %>
<h1>How a score is worked out</h1>
<p class="muted">
  Every figure below is read from <strong><%= @model&.full_name || "no released model" %></strong> as it
  was released, not written down on this page. A different model is a different set of numbers and a
  different answer, which is the point of saying which one produced a score.
  <% if @models.size > 1 %>
    Other released models:
    <%= safe_join(@models.reject { |m| m == @model }.map { |m| link_to m.full_name, scoring_path(model: m.full_name) }, ", ") %>.
  <% end %>
</p>
<div class="card">
  <p><strong>A score is not a measure of truth.</strong> It is what one stated set of rules makes of the
  evidence recorded so far. Change the rules and the number changes; that is why every number here is
  quoted with its model and its snapshot, and why the working is on the page at all. Nothing here is a
  verdict, and none of it is settled until somebody independent has audited the work behind it.</p>
</div>

<h2>The shape of it</h2>
<p>
  Each piece of evidence linked to a claim is weighed on its own, by multiplying a handful of factors.
  The strongest weight in each independent group is kept and the rest are set aside. Those kept weights
  are added to the claim's starting position, and the total is turned into a probability.
</p>
<p class="working-sum">
  <a href="#relevance">relevance</a> × <a href="#observation">observation</a> ×
  <a href="#interpretation">interpretation</a> × <a href="#authenticity">authenticity</a> ×
  <a href="#extraction">extraction</a><% if @config["provenance_factor"] %> × <a href="#provenance">provenance</a><% end %><% if @config["edition_rule"] %> × <a href="#edition">edition</a><% end %>
  = one link's weight
</p>
<p class="working-sum">
  <a href="#prior">prior</a> as log-odds + the <a href="#independence">kept</a> weights =
  posterior log-odds → <a href="#combining">probability</a> → <a href="#states">state</a>
</p>

<h2 id="relevance">Relevance: how directly the passage bears on the claim</h2>
<p>
  Set by whoever recorded the link, and audited like anything else. A passage that states the claim
  outright is worth many times one that merely touches the subject, and context-only evidence is worth
  nothing at all — it is kept because it is worth reading, not because it moves a number.
</p>
<table><tr><th>Relevance</th><th>Factor</th></tr>
  <% (@config["relevance_weight"] || {}).each do |name, value| %>
    <tr><td><%= name %></td><td><%= value %></td></tr>
  <% end %>
</table>

<h2 id="observation">Observation: what kind of thing the evidence is</h2>
<p>
  A measurement and a rumour are not the same evidence, however relevant each is. This factor is where
  that difference lives. <strong>A model's output weighs nothing</strong> — an assistant's opinion is
  never evidence by itself here, however confident it sounds; it may only point at something a person
  or a source said, which is then recorded on its own terms.
</p>
<table><tr><th>Observation</th><th>Factor</th></tr>
  <% (@config["observation_weight"] || {}).each do |name, value| %>
    <tr><td><%= name.tr("_", " ").downcase %></td><td><%= value %><%= " — never evidence on its own" if value.to_f.zero? %></td></tr>
  <% end %>
</table>

<h2 id="interpretation">Interpretation: how many steps of reasoning stand between</h2>
<p>
  A passage that says the thing is worth more than one the reader had to reason from. Each step of
  interpretation costs <strong><%= @config["interpretive_step_penalty"] %></strong>, and the factor
  never goes below nought:
</p>
<p class="working-sum">1 − <%= @config["interpretive_step_penalty"] %> × (number of steps)</p>
<p class="muted">
  So two steps of reasoning on a link is
  1 − <%= @config["interpretive_step_penalty"] %> × 2 =
  <%= format("%.2f", 1 - (@config["interpretive_step_penalty"].to_f * 2)) %>.
</p>

<h2 id="authenticity">Authenticity: whether the passage is really in the source</h2>
<p>
  Whether anyone has checked that the quoted words are actually there, and are really from where they
  are said to be. Unverified is not penalised — most evidence starts that way and penalising it would
  punish recording work honestly — but evidence somebody has looked at and doubted is.
</p>
<table><tr><th>Authenticity</th><th>Factor</th></tr>
  <% (@config["authenticity_factor"] || {}).each do |name, value| %><tr><td><%= name.downcase %></td><td><%= value %></td></tr><% end %>
</table>

<h2 id="extraction">Extraction: whether the reading of the passage is faithful</h2>
<p>
  Whether the evidence says what the passage says, as opposed to what somebody hoped it said. Same
  principle as authenticity: unverified is neutral, and a checked-and-doubtful reading is not.
</p>
<table><tr><th>Extraction</th><th>Factor</th></tr>
  <% (@config["extraction_factor"] || {}).each do |name, value| %><tr><td><%= name.downcase %></td><td><%= value %></td></tr><% end %>
</table>

<% if @config["provenance_factor"] %>
  <h2 id="provenance">Provenance: evidence from the claim's own source</h2>
  <p>
    When a claim was taken out of a document, a passage from <em>that</em> document shows the claim
    quotes its source faithfully. It says nothing about whether the source was right. Treating it as
    support would let any statement corroborate itself simply by being written down.
  </p>
  <table><tr><th>Provenance</th><th>Factor</th></tr>
    <% @config["provenance_factor"].each do |name, value| %>
      <tr><td><%= name.downcase %></td><td><%= value %><%= " — counts for nothing towards the claim being true" if value.to_f.zero? %></td></tr>
    <% end %>
  </table>
<% end %>

<% if @config["edition_rule"] %>
  <h2 id="edition">Edition: which version of the document the claim is about</h2>
  <p>
    A living web page is not one document. If a claim says which edition of a source it is about, a
    reading of a <em>different</em> version of that source weighs nothing against it — an announcement
    revised two days after publication says something the original did not, and counting the revision
    against a claim about the original contradicts something the claim never said. The link is kept and
    shown, marked <code>other edition</code>; it is simply not evidence about this claim.
  </p>
<% end %>

<h2 id="independence">Independence: the same fact, told twice, counts once</h2>
<p>
  Ten outlets repeating one wire report are one piece of evidence, not ten. Evidence is grouped by where
  it independently came from, and in each group, for each direction, <strong>only the strongest weight
  is kept</strong>. The rest appear in the working at nought, marked <code>dependent strongest only</code>,
  with the link that was kept named beside them. Nothing is deleted; it is counted once.
</p>
<p class="muted">Strategy: <%= (@config["independence_strategy"] || "").tr("_", " ") %>.
  <% if @config["independence_fallback"] == "origin" %>
    Evidence nobody has grouped falls back to a group keyed by its origin and the passage itself, so one
    page recorded twice as two sources cannot count twice, while two different passages of one document
    stay distinct.
  <% end %>
</p>

<h2 id="prior">The prior: where a claim starts before any evidence</h2>
<p>
  A claim with no evidence is not true and not false; it starts at even odds, and the arithmetic is done
  in log-odds so that evidence adds rather than multiplies. Causal claims start lower, because "A caused
  B" is a harder thing to establish than "A happened".
</p>
<table><tr><th>Claim type</th><th>Prior</th></tr>
  <% (@config["prior"] || {}).each do |name, value| %><tr><td><%= name.tr("_", " ").downcase %></td><td><%= value %></td></tr><% end %>
</table>
<p class="muted">
  Types not listed are not scored at all: they carry no probability and say why instead — a value
  judgement, a prediction not yet resolved, a rhetorical question. That is a refusal to score, not a
  score of nought.
</p>

<h2 id="combining">Combining: from weights to a probability</h2>
<p>The prior becomes log-odds, the kept weights are added to it, and the total becomes a probability:</p>
<p class="working-sum">probability = 1 ÷ (1 + e<sup>−(prior log-odds + kept weights)</sup>)</p>
<p class="muted">
  Rounding is half-even, at <%= @config.dig("rounding", "weights") %> places for weights,
  <%= @config.dig("rounding", "probability") %> for the probability and
  <%= @config.dig("rounding", "coverage") %> for review coverage. Half-even rather than half-up because
  it does not drift upwards over many roundings.
</p>

<h2 id="states">The state, and when a number is not shown</h2>
<p>A probability becomes a state at these thresholds:</p>
<table><tr><th>From</th><th>State</th></tr>
  <% (@config["state_thresholds"] || {}).each do |name, value| %>
    <tr><td><%= value %></td><td><%= name.sub("_ABOVE", "").tr("_", " ").downcase %></td></tr>
  <% end %>
</table>
<p>
  Two states carry no number at all. <strong>Insufficient evidence</strong> means nothing counted has
  borne on the claim — not that it is doubtful, that nobody has checked. <strong>Not applicable</strong>
  means this model does not score claims of that kind. Both are honest answers, and showing a
  probability for either would be inventing one.
</p>

<h2 id="stability">Stability, and how much the number depends on the settings</h2>
<p>
  The same evidence is scored again with the weights turned down to
  <%= @config.dig("stability", "variants", "conservative") %> and up to
  <%= @config.dig("stability", "variants", "permissive") %> of themselves. The spread between those two
  answers is how much the number is an artefact of the settings rather than of the evidence. A spread
  within <%= @config.dig("stability", "spread_high_max") %> is high stability, within
  <%= @config.dig("stability", "spread_medium_max") %> medium, wider than that low — and fewer than
  <%= @config.dig("stability", "min_groups_for_high") %> independent groups caps it below high however
  narrow the spread.
</p>

<h2 id="coverage">Review coverage: how much of the checking has been done</h2>
<p>
  Always a count of checks, never a percentage of truth. The checks this model declares:
</p>
<ul>
  <% (@config["review_checklist"] || []).each do |check| %><li><%= check.tr("_", " ") %></li><% end %>
</ul>
<p class="muted">
  A check performed by the author of the claim is recorded and shown, and never raises this figure:
  coverage means somebody <em>else</em> has looked.
</p>

<h2>Reading it yourself</h2>
<p>
  Every score carries a trace — the full working, the model, the config hash and the code hash — and the
  same inputs under the same model reproduce it exactly. The
  <%= link_to "specification", docs_path %> sets out the algorithm in full, and a reference
  implementation in Python reproduces every published example, so this node's answers can be checked
  without running this node's code.
</p>
